  (:adr/title "ADR-2607300100: retiring the dead gftd.ai hosts, and standing up ipfs.kotobase.net")
  (:adr/body "# ADR-2607300100: retiring the dead gftd.ai hosts, and standing up ipfs.kotobase.net

**Status**: accepted (all work landed on default branches; two deploys live)
**Date**: 2026-07-30
**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki (owner directive), agent execution

## Context

Six gftd.ai hostnames were confirmed dead and directed for pruning
(owner, 2026-07-29): `gateway.gftd.ai`, `mcp.gftd.ai`, `atproto.gftd.ai`,
`dispatcher.gftd.ai`, `ipfs.gftd.ai`, `llm.murakumo.gftd.ai`. The directive
distinguished two kinds of reference: (a) docs and code presenting a dead host
as current procedure, which get fixed, and (b) historical records, which stay.

Two findings reshaped the work partway through.

**The successor does not replace what the old PDS did.** `atproto.gftd.ai`
moved to `aozora.app`, but `pds.aozora.app` is a fresh, empty atproto PDS:
`com.atproto.sync.listRepos` returns `[]`, and every gftd lexicon —
`ai.gftd.apps.llm.chatCompletions`, `ai.gftd.agent.converse`,
`ai.gftd.cohort.seed` — answers 404 MethodNotImplemented. The old PDS was also
an LLM gateway and a custom-lexicon host; the successor is only a PDS.
Repointing those callers would have replaced a dead host with a live 404.

**One repo owned the hostnames rather than consuming them.** A first pass
generated 155 rewrites in `ai-gftd-apps-gftdcojp` before `wrangler.jsonc`
showed `\"routes\": [\"atproto.gftd.ai/*\", \"mcp.gftd.ai/*\"]` on Worker
`ai-gftd-pds-2603241700`. Its hostname strings are the server naming itself
(`isPds = hostname === \"atproto.gftd.ai\"`, the advertised `hosts:` in
`repo.ts`), so pointing them at a successor is meaningless and, in the routing
predicate, wrong. All 155 were reverted.

## Decision

**Retire rather than repoint, and repoint only where a successor genuinely
serves the same thing.** Concretely:

- `atproto.gftd.ai` / `mcp.gftd.ai` — the Worker was already gone from the
  account (`wrangler deployments list` returns code 10007). Only the routes
  were removed, so a redeploy cannot reclaim the names. The Cloudflare route
  outliving the Worker is why these hostnames resolved in DNS and answered
  530/000.
- LLM callers — routed to `api.murakumo.cloud/v1/chat/completions` directly,
  fulfilling the standing \"murakumo should use murakumo.cloud\" directive. The
  reason it had not happened was that the LLM gateway was reached *through*
  the dead PDS. The PDS hop existed only to dodge CF WAF 1033 on same-account
  subrequests to `llm.gftd.ai`; `api.murakumo.cloud` is off that zone, so the
  reason is gone.
- `ipfs.gftd.ai` — a real successor was built: `ipfs.kotobase.net`.
- Everything with no successor — made honest (503/404/omitted field) rather
  than pointed somewhere plausible.

**`ipfs.gftd.ai` was deliberately not re-bound to the new Worker**, though
both MIGRATION.md Phase 3 and ADR-2607072000 listed it as a route alongside
`ipfs.kotobase.net`. Aiming a retired hostname at a live Worker would undo the
directive that retired it.

## What landed

| repo | commit | change |
|---|---|---|
| gftdcojp/ai-gftd-apps-gftdcojp | 83578ea | removed the two routes; Worker retired |
| gftdcojp/magatama-host-sdk | 34e7e1e | LLM path to api.murakumo.cloud; DID doc stops advertising a dead PDS; xrpc-client origin configurable |
| cloud-itonami/yukkuri | e5e9222 | CLAUDE.md's \"DEFAULT\" LLM path corrected |
| cloud-itonami/syosetsuka | d4c3227 | unreachable hops marked in the architecture flow |
| gftdcojp/chat-shell | 38607fb, d88a872 | dead backends split out of the manifest; dead MCP router default dropped |
| etzhayyim/com-etzhayyim-app-ai-gftd-chat-shell | 5bc0aa7, 8d9adab | same, byte-identical copies |
| gftdcojp/hume | 950d271 | removed a gatewayUrl that could never have fetched |
| etzhayyim/root | aa0a510, 04873c4 | pin-receipt gateway: emptied, then set to ipfs.kotobase.net once it existed |
| network-awai/net-kotobase-ipfs | cba68cf | ipfs.kotobase.net stood up |
| network-awai/net-kotobase | 58eb361 | published retrieval docs corrected + bundles rebuilt |
| com-junkawasaki/root | 5c697a5 | ADR-2607072000 moved to live |

Deploys: `net-kotobase-ipfs` version bd9548ab (new), `net-kotobase` version
4f121668 (redeploy so the corrected llms.txt actually ships — those files are
compile-time inlined via shadow.resource rc/inline).

## ipfs.kotobase.net

Live, verified: `/health` 200; `/ipfs/QmYwAPJzv5CZsnA625s3Xf2nemtYgPpHdWEz79ojWnPbdG`
200 with 254,355 bytes in 0.15s, byte-identical to ipfs.io and kotobase.net;
`/ipns/en.wikipedia-on-ipfs.org` 200; `/ipfs/not-a-cid` 502.

The Worker was already written and tested (ADR-2607072000); only `routes` was
commented out. B2 is deliberately left unconfigured — upstream still runs
`KOTOBASE_B2_ARCHIVE_CIDS=0`, so the bucket is empty and read credentials
would buy a miss round-trip per request while serving nothing. Every hit takes
the `KOTOBASE_IPFS_GATEWAY_URL` fallback (`https://ipfs.io`, chosen over
dweb.link and w3s.link because those 301 to a per-CID subdomain first while
ipfs.io answers 200 directly in 0.08s; cloudflare-ipfs.com is dead and
trustless-gateway.link 406s a plain GET).

**This means retrieval latency and availability currently ride on the public
IPFS network, not on kotobase's own storage.** For a caller that is not a
degraded mode — a pinned CID is on the swarm, which is what makes it fetchable
at all — but it is a real dependency and is stated as such in the published
docs. Turning archiving on upstream and setting `KOTOBASE_B2_*` promotes B2 to
the first tier with no change to the hostname or its callers.

No DNS step was needed: `custom_domain: true` plus `workers_routes (write)`
was enough for Cloudflare to create the record. The token holds `zone (read)`
only and could not have written one directly.

## Documentation that was wrong

Four published or authoritative claims were false and are corrected:

- `kotobase.net/llms.txt` and `/llms-full.txt` told every reader to resolve
  CIDs at `ipfs.gftd.ai` (530). These are served by the Worker, so it was live
  misinformation.
- `net-kotobase/docs/USING-AS-A-PIN-SERVICE.md` said the Worker's own
  `GET /ipfs/<cid>` returns 404 \"not archived and no gateway configured\" in
  production. It returns 200 with real content; `/ipfs/not-a-cid` returns 502
  \"archive gateway unavailable\" — the repo's own string. A gateway URL was
  already configured in production.
- `net-kotobase-ipfs`'s `gateway.cljs` docstring claimed no
  `KOTOBASE_IPFS_GATEWAY_URL` was configured \"anywhere in this org\". It was.
  What was missing was the hostname, not the capability.
- `MIGRATION.md`'s redirect-keep policy said legacy hosts stay resolvable as
  301/alias and that references should not be rewritten. Still true of
  `kotobase.gftd.ai` (200); false of `ipfs.gftd.ai` (530), which is neither
  redirect nor alias. The note now says which is which, because \"leave the
  references alone\" is right for one and wrong for the other.

## Verification

- `net-kotobase-ipfs`: 11 tests / 34 assertions, 0 failures (re-run after the
  docstring edit).
- `etzhayyim/root` ipfs-pinner: 22/22 (node --test), twice — once for the
  removal, once for the ipfs.kotobase.net default.
- `gftdcojp/hume`: 6 tests / 27 assertions, 0 failures (kbb -M:test).
- `net-kotobase`: `[:worker]` 97 files 0 warnings; `[:entry]` 155 files 12
  warnings, all `:infer-warning` externs inference and pre-existing — this
  change touched only .txt and .md, so it cannot have introduced one. Bundle
  diff was exactly 10 lines (5 old-host removals, 5 new-host additions), which
  also confirms the checked-in bundles were in sync with main's source.
- `chat-shell` x2: both wrangler.jsonc still parse (vars 7 to 5); tsc clean on
  the rewritten handler apart from implicit-any from `./$types` being
  unresolvable outside a SvelteKit build.
- Post-deploy smoke: kotobase.net `/`, `/health`, `/.well-known/did.json`,
  `/llms.txt`, `/ipfs/<cid>` all 200; kotobase.gftd.ai 200;
  ipfs.kotobase.net/health 200.

**Not verified**: `magatama-host-sdk`'s vitest suite. `@etzhayyim/xrpc` is a
`workspace:*` dependency whose workspace root is `ai-gftd-apps-gftdcojp`, which
an isolated worktree cannot assemble; the typecheck there ran with imports
unresolved. **Also not verified**: the B2 code path in `net-kotobase-ipfs` —
with no credentials set, `b2-configured?` is false in production, so that
branch has only ever run against the unit tests' fake `fetch`.

## Open, needing an owner decision

1. **A credential sits in plaintext in an archive.** `m365-archive`'s Teams
   export contains an org-shared API key (`sk_live_org_3a8f…`) posted to a
   channel. The host it authenticated is dead, so it is probably inert, but
   the archive is immutable by policy and this is a rotation decision, not an
   edit.
2. **`jp-ashiba` looks retired but is not archived.** No Worker on the account
   under either name, `jp-ashiba.gftd.ai` does not connect. Its
   `actor-manifest.jsonld` `@context` points at `atproto.gftd.ai/contexts/...`
   (404 at the successor). Fixing a dead actor's manifest is low value; the
   real question is whether to archive the repo.
3. **`ai-gftd-arms` DoDAF diagrams and `ai-gftd-apps-gftdcojp` lexicon
   descriptions** still name the dead hosts. Judged category (b) — they
   describe a system as it was — and deliberately left.
4. **Turning on B2 archiving** in net-kotobase is the thing that makes
   ipfs.kotobase.net independent of the public IPFS network. Its own
   `docs/ENVIRONMENT.md` requires a separate ADR for that.

## Resume point

Everything above is on default branches; nothing is parked on a branch or in a
worktree. The next concrete step, if item 4 is wanted:

```bash
# confirm the current state rather than trusting this ADR
curl -s https://ipfs.kotobase.net/_app/meta          # {\"b2_configured\":false,...}
gh api repos/network-awai/net-kotobase/contents/worker/wrangler.jsonc \\
  --jq '.content' | base64 -d | grep KOTOBASE_B2_ARCHIVE_CIDS
```

Then: write the ADR that `docs/ENVIRONMENT.md` requires, flip
`KOTOBASE_B2_ARCHIVE_CIDS=1`, backfill or accept forward-only archiving, and
set `KOTOBASE_B2_{ENDPOINT,BUCKET,KEY_ID,APP_KEY}` on `net-kotobase-ipfs` with
a read-only key. The gateway hostname and every caller stay unchanged.

## Third wave: `murakumo.gftd.ai` (2026-09-12)

Same owner directive (\"murakumo.gftd.ai を prune\"), one more hostname. Unlike
the six above it had **no DNS record at all** — the 192.0.2.1 answer comes from
the `*.gftd.ai → 192.0.2.1` (proxied) wildcard sinkhole every retired host
inherits (DNS queries of a record-less Workers route answer the same
placeholder). What was still alive was a zombie: route `murakumo.gftd.ai/*` and
script `ai-gftd-murakumo-2603241700` (same 2603241700 deployment wave as the
retired PDS), holding a `*/5 * * * *` cron trigger whose ~1811 invocations per
7d were pure cron burn, and bindings pointing at a host this ADR already
retired (`mcp.gftd.ai`) plus `murakumo-serve.gftd.ai`. No manifest repo, no
other route consuming the script. Both deleted via Cloudflare API (route id
`b62291183c804cd7883cbcd294f6c3c2`; script with `force=true`, same cron-blocked
delete as the wave-2 PDS). Verified after: script answers 10007, the zone has
no murakumo route left, and the hostname answers exactly like `mcp.gftd.ai` /
`atproto.gftd.ai`.

Repo follow-up, category (a): `70-tools/bmc/collect.cljk` tracked the script as
the `:cloud-murakumo` product's worker; removed with the reason inline. The
1811/7d was cron burn, not product traffic — murakumo.cloud is served by
`murakumo-llms` (the zone's only worker routes) and `api.murakumo.cloud`, both
verified 200 after the delete. The two ADRs naming the host stay as history.

## Fourth wave: the remaining zombie routes and retired-host DNS (2026-09-12)

Same owner directive extended to the whole zone: audit every gftd.ai route and
DNS record, prune what is clearly dead, leave anything down-but-not-retired
alone. The audit walked the zone's DNS records, Workers routes and the
account's tunnels; it found five more zombies — null-script routes, the exact
shape the third wave defined — and three CNAME leftovers pointing retired
hosts at tunnels. All eight deleted via the Cloudflare API, each verified by a
post-delete GET returning not-found.

Routes deleted (script binding null; the route outlived its script):

- `monitor.gftd.ai/*` — `50f59dfce26d4c69b87e8ff3c80ea2cc`
- `jacob-status.gftd.ai/*` — `bb191ae087284180846c8a68425eec86`
- `kotoba-backend.gftd.ai/*` — `86dc6bfddb8a4a65b89fe12cbe739bd8`
- `explorer.gftd.ai/*` — `a40d7cecba684fb3ac6b68139505fd41`
- `lg-manimani.gftd.ai/*` — `de68f6b8a7d946bb948e177a0e5b4201`

Zone routes: 30 → 25, every remaining route script-bound (`kotobase.gftd.ai →
net-kotobase` among them). Zone DNS records: 94 → 91.

CNAME leftovers deleted (hosts the first wave retired; the DNS record was what
survived them):

- `gateway.gftd.ai` → `<uuid>.cfargotunnel.com`, record
  `c0b4beda27c703bd3ed9197225d7083b` (created 2026-07-09). The gftd-llm
  tunnel behind it is healthy — the host was retired, not the tunnel, so the
  record was re-binding a retired name to a live tunnel.
- `ipfs.gftd.ai` — record `b6cff21a756a2956195dac77b93050a8` (2026-05-28), and
  `ipfs-origin.gftd.ai` — `c02b5837270c42735f595fea2902c6b3` (2026-04-26),
  both to the ipfs-origin tunnel (down).

**A measurement finding that recolors every curl number in this ADR**: the
zone's authoritative nameservers answer literal record contents to the public.
A tunnel CNAME resolves to `<uuid>.cfargotunnel.com`, which has no public A
record and answers AAAA `fd10:aec2:5dae::` — a ULA no outside client can reach
— and a Workers custom domain answers its literal `AAAA 100::`. Plain
curl/dig therefore returns 000/unreachable for every gftd.ai hostname,
including live ones, and **000 is not evidence that a route or tunnel is
dead**. Liveness truth here is the Cloudflare API (route/script binding,
tunnel connections) plus edge-level tests (`curl --resolve
<host>:443:<murakumo.cloud edge IP>`), which during this wave gave
kotobase.gftd.ai 200 (live route), engi.gftd.ai 404 at / (live custom domain),
monitor.gftd.ai 530 (zombie since deleted). The earlier readings in this ADR
(530/000) and the July kotobase.gftd.ai 200 are edge-level or
client-environment artifacts, not facts about the zone.

Category (a) follow-up: `orgs/com-junkawasaki/tanabe-3d/marble_pipeline.py`
was still emitting retrieval URLs to the retired `ipfs.gftd.ai`; repointed to
the first wave's successor `https://ipfs.kotobase.net/ipfs/<cid>`, and its
Stage-2 permanent pin (`ai.gftd.apps.ipfsIngest.pin` through the gftd CLI)
— aimed at the retired gftd PDS, whose lexicons answer 404 at the successor —
now skips with that reason instead of attempting a doomed call. The one other
reference outside ADRs,
`90-docs/business/net-kotobase-graphdb-baas-execution-plan-2026-07-24.md`, is
a dated measurement snapshot (530 was true when measured) and stays. The
search covers checked-out files and the repo-search index; west repos that are
not checked out were not searched — the honest bound of this audit.

Left alone deliberately — **down is not retired**, and no owner decision
retires any of these:

- `murakumo-fleet` tunnel (0 connections), serving the fleet's litellm /
  murakumo-serve / `murakumo-{levi,naphtali,simeon}` / dashboard / xrpc
  `*.murakumo-internal` surface. Dark while the tunnel is down;
  `api.murakumo.cloud` is the live fleet surface (root answers 404, alive).
- `ollama-tunnel.gftd.ai` CNAME → the down ollama-gftd tunnel (record from
  2026-04-15). If the tunnel returns, the hostname works again.
- `engi` / `itad` / `kakure` Workers custom domains: edge-live, publicly
  unresolvable under the literal-answer behavior above.

Also not verified from here: the tunnels are locally managed
(`remote_config: false`), so their ingress rules are not readable via the API
— whether a down tunnel would resume serving its hostname is a fact about the
machine, not about this account.

## Fifth wave: the `kotobase.gftd.ai` CNAME (2026-09-12)

Same owner directive (`kotobase.gftd.ai を prune`). One record deleted, one
route deliberately kept — the split matters because the newest decision on
this hostname is not this ADR's.

**Deleted**: the proxied CNAME `kotobase.gftd.ai →
fe8d39eb-07fd-4760-a8e7-0938035a0b2a.cfargotunnel.com` (record
`88cf1e94e3714ade2cb1e1cf343dd322`, created 2026-06-02). Pre-delete GET
confirmed the exact content, DELETE returned 200, post-GET returned
`81044: Record does not exist`. Zone DNS records: 91 → 90. The kotobase
tunnel itself stays (down, 0 connections — down is not retired). Zone routes
stay at 25: `kotobase.gftd.ai/* → net-kotobase` remains.

**Why the route stays**: ADR-2608179000 (2026-08-17) found the abolished host
answering 200 with the whole site and corrected it to the documented contract
— browsers 301 to kotobase.net while `/.well-known/did.json` keeps serving,
because `did:web:kotobase.gftd.ai` resolves at that path and an abolished
name still owes answers to the identities issued under it. Its gap ledger
records the route as deliberately remaining, removal bundled with a DID
migration plan; ADR-2608051000's removal-everything decision (owner,
2026-08-04) is sequenced behind that plan. Re-measured live today at the
edge, both murakumo.cloud edge addresses: did.json 200 with
`alsoKnownAs [did:web:kotobase.net]` (the legacy document — id
`did:web:kotobase.gftd.ai` — still served), browser navigation to /signup
301 → kotobase.net/signup. Nothing in this wave touched the route, the
Worker, or the repo-side legacy-DID items.

**Measured before and after the delete — the fourth wave's prediction was
wrong.** Pre-delete, plain DNS answered the proxied CNAME's literal tunnel
chain (`<uuid>.cfargotunnel.com`, AAAA `fd10:aec2:5dae::`, no A): 000.
Post-delete, authoritative (everton/vivienne.ns.cloudflare.com) and 1.1.1.1
both answer `A 192.0.2.1` — the proxied wildcard record's LITERAL content
(TEST-NET-1, unreachable), not Cloudflare edge IPs; plain curl stays 000
(timeout), identical to pre-delete. The fourth wave predicted that deleting
the CNAME would hand the name to the wildcard whose edge IPs plus the live
route would restore 200 — refuted: the wildcard is itself a proxied record,
and this zone's proxied records answer literally too. The literal-answer
behavior now covers A, AAAA and CNAME, proxied or not. At the edge
(`curl --resolve` to the murakumo.cloud edge addresses) /health returned 200
on BOTH addresses before and after the delete. Net effect on clients: none —
publicly the name was already unreachable before the delete (dead tunnel
chain) and stays unreachable after (192.0.2.1); MIGRATION.md's keep-alias
note (200 measured 2026-07-29, an edge-level fact per the fourth wave)
remains true at that layer.

Relative to ADR-2608051000's six-item scope, this wave completed item ⑥ (the
CF DNS record; item ⑤ was already done earlier); items ①-④ remain gated by
ADR-2608179000's DID migration plan and were not touched.
")))
